%I A401169 #17 Oct 25 2026 21:01:34
%S A401169 1,2,3,5,21,55,610,987,832040,267914296
%N A401169 A quadratic form represents every Fibonacci number iff it represents these ten numbers.
%C A401169 More formally: The minimal Fibonacci-universality criterion for positive-definite, integer-matrix quadratic forms.
%C A401169 A positive-definite quadratic form with integral Gram matrix represents every Fibonacci number if and only if it represents every term of this sequence. Here a quadratic  form has the shape Q(x) = x^T A x, where A is a symmetric positive-definite integer matrix, and representation means Q(x) = m for some integer vector x.
%C A401169 This is a Fibonacci analog of the Conway-Schneeberger Fifteen Theorem (see Conway and Bhargava). That theorem states that a form in the same class represents every positive integer if and only if it represents 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 7, 10, 14, and 15 (A030050). The present criterion replaces the target set of all positive integers by the Fibonacci numbers. Representing every Fibonacci number is strictly weaker than representing every positive integer, as the example below demonstrates, yet because of the sparsity of the Fibonacci sequence, the associated criterion set contains much larger values.
%C A401169 Kim, Kim, and Oh proved that every set S of positive integers has a finite criterion subset: representing that subset forces a positive-definite quadratic form with integral Gram matrix to represent all of S. Kim, Lee, and Oh established uniqueness of the inclusion-minimal criterion for integer targets; Kala, Krásenský, and Romeo developed the corresponding theory over totally real number fields. Despite these general existence results, explicit minimal criteria for nontrivial infinite target sets have been determined in relatively few cases.
%C A401169 These ten numbers constitute the unique inclusion-minimal Fibonacci criterion set. More strongly, every set of Fibonacci numbers whose representation forces representation of all Fibonacci numbers must contain all ten terms. For each term t, there exists a positive-definite quadratic form with integral Gram matrix representing every Fibonacci number except t.
%C A401169 With F(0) = 0 and F(1) = 1, the terms are F(k) for k = 1, 3, 4, 5, 8, 10, 15, 16, 30, 42.
%C A401169 Consequently, representing F(1), ..., F(42) suffices to represent every Fibonacci number, and the endpoint 42 is optimal.
%D A401169 Manjul Bhargava, On the Conway-Schneeberger Fifteen Theorem, in Quadratic Forms and Their Applications, Contemporary Mathematics 272, American Mathematical Society, 2000, pp. 27-37.
%D A401169 John H. Conway, Universal quadratic forms and the Fifteen Theorem, in Quadratic Forms and Their Applications, Contemporary Mathematics 272, American Mathematical Society, 2000, pp. 23-26.
%D A401169 Vítězslav Kala, Jakub Krásenský, and Giuliano Romeo, Universality criterion sets for quadratic forms over number fields, Advances in Mathematics 500 (2026), Article 111080.
%D A401169 Byeong Moon Kim, Myung-Hwan Kim, and Byeong-Kweon Oh, A finiteness theorem for representability of quadratic forms by forms, Journal für die reine und angewandte Mathematik 581 (2005), pp. 23-30.
%D A401169 Kyoungmin Kim, Jeongwon Lee, and Byeong-Kweon Oh, Minimal universality criterion sets on the representations of binary quadratic forms, Journal of Number Theory 238 (2022), pp. 37-59.
%D A401169 Scott Duke Kominers, A Fibonacci Analogue of the Fifteen Theorem, preprint, October 12, 2026, Theorem 1.1.
%H A401169 OEIS Wiki, <a href="https://oeis.org/wiki/A400K_Winners">A400K Winners</a>, 2026.
%e A401169 The quadratic form Q(x,y,z,w) = x^2 + y^2 + z^2 + 55*w^2 represents all ten criterion values:
%e A401169   Q(1,0,0,0) = 1;
%e A401169   Q(1,1,0,0) = 2;
%e A401169   Q(1,1,1,0) = 3;
%e A401169   Q(2,1,0,0) = 5;
%e A401169   Q(4,2,1,0) = 21;
%e A401169   Q(0,0,0,1) = 55;
%e A401169   Q(24,5,3,0) = 610;
%e A401169   Q(31,5,1,0) = 987;
%e A401169   Q(912,14,10,0) = 832040;
%e A401169   Q(16362,444,46,0) = 267914296.
%e A401169 The criterion therefore implies that Q represents every Fibonacci number. However, Q does not represent all integers; in particular, it does not represent 7 (such a representation would require w = 0, and 7 is not a sum of three integer squares). Thus Q satisfies the Fibonacci criterion but fails the Fifteen Theorem criterion (cf. A030050).
%e A401169 The form x^2 + y^2 + 2z^2 represents every positive Fibonacci number smaller than F(42) = 267914296, but does not represent F(42). Its positive exceptions are precisely the integers 4^r(16s + 14), where r and s are nonnegative integers; F(42) = 4(16*4186160 + 14) is the first Fibonacci number of this shape.
%Y A401169 Cf. A000045 (Fibonacci numbers), A030050 (Fifteen Theorem criterion set), A030051 (290-Theorem criterion set), A116582 (odd-universality criterion), A154363 (prime-universality criterion), A304204 (universality criterion for sums of generalized pentagonal numbers), A304206 (universality criterion for sums of generalized octagonal numbers).
